<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Djemal Pasha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1872">1872</a>–<a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Ahmed Djemal Pasha, Cemal Pasha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Djemal Pasha was an Ottoman officer and politician, one of the three Young Turk leaders who governed the empire during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. As commander in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> from 1914 he led failed attacks on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a>, abolished the autonomy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a> and executed Arab nationalists in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, earning the name 'the Butcher'. He fled after the war and was assassinated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> in 1922.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/djemal-pasha.jpg" alt="Djemal Pasha" width="320" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Djemal_Pasha2.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ahmed Djemal was born on 6 May 1872 in Mytilene on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a>, the son of Mehmed Nesib Bey, a military pharmacist. He graduated from the Kuleli Military High School in 1890 and from the staff college in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> in 1893. Posted to the II Army Corps in 1896 and later to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Salonica</a> frontier, he came to sympathise with the Committee of Union and Progress, a secret reformist party, and joined it in 1898. After the Young Turk Revolution of 1908 he sat on its central committee. He governed the Adana Vilayet from 1909 to 1911, where he helped Armenian victims of the Adana massacres and was praised by Christian missionaries as a competent administrator, and he served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Djemal became Minister of the Ottoman Navy in February 1914 and, with Talaat and Enver, one of the Three Pashas who effectively ruled the empire during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. He had first favoured an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. In October 1914 he authorised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> strike that brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and France into war against the empire. In 1915 he was given full military and civil powers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. His two attacks on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a> failed, and wartime hardship and natural disasters turned the population against Ottoman rule.</p><p>Between 1915 and 1916 he had 34 Syrian and Lebanese politicians and nationalists executed, among them the men hanged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a> on 6 May 1916. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> called him as-Saffah, the Butcher. Some people in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> praised him for a water pipeline that saved the city from drought in 1917. Historians contest his role in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenian-genocide">Armenian genocide</a>: he favoured forced assimilation and conversion, and his policies allowed some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> to survive. Wolfgang Gust says he preserved perhaps 150,000 lives while helping to kill another 150,000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the empire's defeat in October 1918 Djemal fled with other leaders of the Committee to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, and a military court sentenced him to death in absentia. In 1920 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> as a military adviser to the Afghan army. He was assassinated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tiflis</a> on 21 July 1922 by members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenian-revolutionary-federation">Armenian Revolutionary Federation</a>, as part of Operation Nemesis, a plan to kill the surviving chief perpetrators of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenian-genocide">Armenian genocide</a>. He was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzurum">Erzurum</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> 6 May is Martyrs' Day, a national holiday marking the executions of 1916, and the squares where they took place were renamed Martyrs' Square.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Djemal Pasha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The First World War closed the age with censorship, famine in Mount Lebanon and, in 1915–1916, the hanging of Arab nationalists in Beirut and Damascus under <b>Djemal Pasha</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/nahda">Nahda · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Talaat Pasha and <b>Cemal Pasha</b> were assassinated in exile during Operation Nemesis, an Armenian revenge operation: Soghomon Tehlirian, whose family had been killed in the genocide, shot Talaat in Berlin and was acquitted by a German jury.</blockquote><a href="/movement/young-turks">Young Turks · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1915 <b>Jamal Pasha</b> ended the autonomy; wartime blockade and famine killed a large part of the population.</blockquote><a href="/state/mount-lebanon-mutasarrifate">Mount Lebanon Mutasarrifate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mytilene, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.11,26.55&amp;year=1904" aria-label="Mytilene on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Djemal Pasha"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4771 4221h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4771 4221h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.11,26.55&amp;year=1904">What was here in 1904? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
